{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the birth of facebook and the complex web of ambition, betrayal, and genius that surrounded its creation. directed by david fincher with razor-sharp precision, the movie is not just a biopic—it’s a modern tragedy wrapped in the sleek, cold aesthetics of the digital age. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as a brilliant yet emotionally stunted prodigy whose social ineptitude masks a ruthless drive for validation and control. his performance is hauntingly accurate, capturing the quiet intensity and detached demeanor that made zuckerberg both a visionary and a pariah.\n\nthe screenplay by aaron sorkin is nothing short of brilliant. every line crackles with wit, tension, and intellectual heft, transforming legal depositions and coding sessions into gripping drama. sorkin’s signature rapid-fire dialogue never feels forced; instead, it propels the narrative forward with the momentum of a thriller. the film’s structure—interweaving two lawsuits with the rise of facebook—is a narrative triumph, allowing the audience to piece together the truth from conflicting testimonies, mirroring the ambiguity of online identity and reputation.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally stellar. jesse plemons as justin timberlake’s character, sean parker, brings a magnetic, chaotic energy that perfectly embodies the early internet’s wild, unregulated spirit. andrew garfield as eduardo saverin is heartbreaking in his quiet devastation, embodying the loyal friend betrayed by ambition. the chemistry between the leads is electric, and their fractured relationships form the emotional core of the film.\n\ntrent reznor and atticus ross’s score is revolutionary—a haunting blend of electronic pulses and melancholic strings that feels both futuristic and deeply human. it doesn’t just accompany the film; it becomes its heartbeat, underscoring the loneliness beneath the digital facade. the cinematography, led by jeff cronenweth, uses cool tones and tight framing to evoke a world where connection is algorithmic and intimacy is commodified.\n\nbeneath the surface, the film asks profound questions about ownership, friendship, and the cost of innovation. is zuckerberg a villain or a victim of his own genius? does success justify betrayal? the movie refuses easy answers, leaving viewers unsettled and contemplative long after the credits roll.\n\nthe social network is more than a movie about a website—it’s a chilling portrait of how technology reshapes human relationships, and how the pursuit of connection in the digital age can lead to profound isolation. it’s intelligent, urgent, and unforgettable.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.9073468138230965e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0005539313424378633}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.3604399859905243}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.3138180375099182}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0009132028790190816}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.004028658382594585}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -7.295342220459133e-05}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -1.3451426029205322}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.005944904405623674}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": " birth", "logprob": -2.626603603363037}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00013040646445006132}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0003219324571546167}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.017940977588295937}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -3.933898824470816e-06}, {"token": " complex", "logprob": -0.771970272064209}, {"token": " web", "logprob": -0.8203262686729431}, {"to